## Logistics overhaul (planned, to discuss in detail later)
- **Aircraft replacements cost resources:** replacing lost aircraft needs supplies, fuel and engineering, not just time.
- **Garrisons need supply to survive:** a location without supply slowly loses troops until it is resupplied. Done in v0.22 (tested).
- **AI builds and supplies its empire (supplying part done in v0.22: the AI sends convoys to low-supply outposts; building up its locations is still planned):** the AI creates and resupplies new locations and builds up the ones it owns (port, airfield, troops).
- **More places to capture:** add more locations to the campaign maps, including ones that can hold an air wing.
- **Stationed ships:** station a group of ships at a location. If that location is invaded, a battle starts between the invasion force and the defending "port ship" force.
- **Advanced logistics system (days-left readout and daily upkeep done in v0.22, tested) (L):** troops use up supply at their location every day (in the base game they only use supply while fighting). A readout on the location panel shows how much supply the garrison needs per day and **how many days the current supply will last**, with a warning when it runs low. Builds on "Garrisons need supply to survive" above. Base game facts checked in the code: `MapLocation.supplies[faction]` is the stock, `CampaignData.suppliesUsedPerDayFighting` is spent only inside `CampaignManager.CheckTroopEngagements` (when both sides have troops there), and `CampaignManager.GetTroopStrength(troops, supplies)` already makes troops weaker when supplies are below troops. So the days-left number and the daily upkeep are new; the stock and the weakening already exist.
- **Aircraft need supplies to be rebuilt (checked in the base game code):** yes, but it is optional. Replacing lost aircraft happens in `CampaignManager.ReplenishLostAircraft` at the start of air ops each day. When the campaign setting `usePortResourcesToResupplyAirfields` is ON, each replaced aircraft costs troops, supplies, engineering and fuel from that location, taken from the aircraft's own `resourceCost` (a 4-number list in its unit file, `UnitListData.resourceCost`). If the location can't pay, the aircraft is not replaced and the player gets a warning naming the location. When the setting is OFF, lost aircraft come back for free (all at once, or a limited number per day if `replenishLimitAircraft` is on). Aircraft carried on ships do the same with `usePortResourcesToResupplyShipAircraft` (`ResupplyShips.GetShipAircraftCost`). Still to find out: what the shipped `resourceCost` numbers are for each aircraft, and which setting the mod's campaigns have on. Work to do: make the cost show on the air panel and in the logistics readout (L, part of the logistics system). Aircraft flight work itself stays on hold.
- **New historical map locations (planned) (M-L):** more locations on the map that the player can fight for (islands, atolls, bases, ports that mattered in the real war), each with a historical start owner, garrison and port / airfield level. Overlaps with "More places to capture" above.
- **Custom objectives and new events (planned) (M-L):** campaign objectives that are more than "take this place": for example a recon mission of a certain area, escort a convoy, hold a location for some days. New events such as being ordered to scout an area and reporting back. Needs the game's mission and event code checked (`CampaignMission`, `CampaignEvent`) before the effort is known.
- **Sickness event (requested by Aaron, planned, M):** a random campaign event where disease spreads through a task force's crews. The ships' crews become ill and their efficiency drops (slower reloads, repairs and damage control, like the short-crew effects in v0.18.2) for a set period of time, then recover. Needs the crew efficiency code in `crews.md` checked first. Tag `[SICK]`.
- **Recon system (planned) (M-L):** scout planes with no loadout can go to a location and "scout it". When they arrive the map shows how many enemy forces are there (ships, troops, aircraft), with how old that information is. Pairs with the custom recon objectives above and with the existing scout automation. Aircraft work is on hold, so this starts on the campaign map side only (the report and the display) and flying details come later.
